Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  tomorrow is the 10th anniversary of the  night that trayvon martin was killed you  remember this story the 17 year old  teenager was gunned down on his way home  by neighborhood watch volunteer george  zimmerman who was found not guilty of  second-degree murder  the cbs reports one hour special trayvon  martin ten years later is what we're  calling it explores a legacy in the  impact of his death it features  interviews with his mother sabrina  fulton and other mothers of the movement  whose children have been killed by  police or gun violence and those on the  front line of the social justice  movement we visited the annual peace  walk held in trayvon's honor and this  year it happened to fall on what would  have been his 27th birthday  [Music]  what did you think when you saw the  crowd today  i think i was good when i woke up this  morning i prayed i  meditated and so i was feeling really  really  like excited and really ready and really  excited but then when i drove in the  gate and i saw so many people and i'm  just  it just made me tear up  [Music]  it made me tear up and it made me  realize  the impact of trayvon martin  ten years ago sabrina fulton became the  mother of a movement  we got to see past  what's in front of us  we can't give up her son trayvon martin  was just 17 years old when he was gunned  down while walking back to his father's  house from the convenience store he was  shot by george zimmerman a neighborhood  watch volunteer who was 28. the pain is  still fresh  the hurt is still fresh the  disappointment and sadness is still  fresh so it seems like it happened  recently  and i have to remind myself that it's  been 10 years this year an annual peace  walk and peace talk held in trayvon  martin's hometown miami florida fell on  what would have been his 27th birthday i  have to say every time i look at that  it's now an iconic photo of him ms hoody  i see your face  i see you when i look at him what do you  see when you look at him  i see at times pain  i see  i still see trey  i still see  my baby boy  but there is something to smile about  you know i'm grateful that i did have  him for 17 years  but  the pain and hurt and bitterness of him  not still being here  is something that i carry with me every  day  i've heard you say that you turned your  pain into purpose what do you mean when  this first happened the tragedy of my  son i can absolutely say that  i never thought i would be happy again i  never thought i would smile again and i  asked god for strength because that's  what i heard other people saying about  me  how strong i was it didn't happen  overnight but he made me strong it  happened sabrina fulton realized there  was power in sisterhood she created the  circle of mothers for women who have  lost children or family members to  senseless gun violence  when sabrina did the circle of mothers  i remember i became so broken but i left  so empowered it's been a sisterhood that  we have formed together to encourage  each other to support each other to cry  with each other they're still shooting  us they're still killing us but we are  still visible we are still telling them  that we're not quitting this is our life  now  boy you can watch trayvon martin 10  years later on the cbs news streaming  network and the smithsonian channel  that's tomorrow at eight o'clock eastern  and you can also watch it on monday on  bet at eight o'clock eastern also those  channels are part of our parent company  paramount  see all those mothers standing together  really talks about the scope of what's  going on in this country you know i  wonder what they told you about how for  so long these types of cases would end  with acquittals and then now we have  recent convictions like in the case of  ahmad arbury and george floyd what do  they say about that so much has changed  but so much has also stayed the same i  know and i asked that very question  adriana because they said  listen we are very happy about what  happened in those two cases but sabrina  called it bittersweet she said you take  two steps forward and then you take two  steps back and what it took to get to  those acquittals and there's still as  she said a lot of injustice that happens  that people are not being held  accountable same with tamika tamika who  is brianna taylor's mother she is  certainly one of those people who  doesn't feel that she has gotten justice  in her daughter's death so that while  they welcome those verdicts of course  they still know that there's still so  much work to be done and people are  still not being held accountable in the  way that they would like to see  yeah so much uh happened in these 10  years and i i hope the documentary gets  into the comments from president obama  you'll recall uh back in 2012 lebron  james put a hoodie on in solidarity with  with trayvon martin that was a big part  of the story  we get into all of that we have an  interview with eric holder as you know  was the attorney general at the time but  i keep thinking guys about sabrina  fulton she never asked for this and now  she's a public speaker something that  she she grabs but it's certainly not  where she wants to be  we'll be right back

